# A timer left un-dismissed on the alarm screen for a while - confirms the
# live elapsed-overtime counter actually counts up rather than freezing or
# showing something broken. (Forcing TS_RUNNING into overtime directly via
# SetTimerRemaining was considered instead, but sweep_expiries() - called
# right after every SetTimerIndex mutation specifically to "catch an
# immediate overtime" - would immediately flag it and show the alarm
# screen anyway, so there's no reachable "quiet overtime row sitting in
# the list" state to test separately from this one.)
#
# Explicitly UNFREEZES the display right after import: wipe_and_prep.seq
# now freezes it by default for every sequence (see that file's own
# comment) - this is the one deliberate exception, since freezing would
# defeat the entire point here (watching the counter actually tick in
# real time is what's being tested, not a static value).
#
# NOTE: live-verified (2026-09-27). This file previously (briefly) showed
# the live overtime counter jumping BACKWARD between its two screenshots
# (e.g. "+0:04" -> "+0:01") in the containerized harness, while the exact
# same button/AppMessage sequence run natively always ticked correctly and
# monotonically. Root-caused to the container harness's OLD FAKETIME
# setup, NOT anything in this app: every separate `pebble` CLI invocation
# (screenshot/send-app-message/emu-button/...) is a fresh OS process, and
# pebble-tool's own PebbleTransportEmulator.post_connect() unconditionally
# resyncs the emulator's clock (a SetUTC packet stamped with that
# process's own time.time()) on every single connect. The container's old
# FAKETIME="@2020-01-01 12:00:00" (an absolute "start-at" value) anchors
# its "keep advancing" reference to WHEN THE AFFECTED PROCESS ITSELF
# STARTED (confirmed against libfaketime's own README), so each new,
# short-lived `pebble` CLI process reported a time near the pinned instant
# plus its own (tiny) uptime - repeatedly discarding real elapsed time
# between separate commands, not just occasionally drifting.
#
# Fixed at the source (tests/functional/docker/run-sequence-in-container.sh):
# FAKETIME is now a plain RELATIVE OFFSET (no "@"), which libfaketime
# evaluates fresh against the real system clock on every query by every
# process, with no per-process anchor - see that script's own comment for
# the full mechanism and the fix's live verification (this file's counter
# now correctly and monotonically increases, e.g. "+0:04" -> "+0:10").
#
# Residual, much milder caveat: the two live "+MM:SS" screenshots below
# still aren't guaranteed byte-for-byte reproducible run to run, since the
# exact real wall-clock overhead of the intervening `pebble` CLI calls
# (process spawn/connect time) still varies - but the counter should
# always be monotonically increasing and roughly track the scripted SLEEP
# durations now. A future golden diff confined to the "+MM:SS" text region
# with a plausible (close to the scripted SLEEP total), forward-only value
# is expected drift, not evidence of a regression - but a value that goes
# DOWN, or is wildly off, is a real regression signal again (unlike
# before this fix, when a backward jump on its own proved nothing).
# Everything else this file checks (the alarm screen appearing at all,
# its layout, the Stop button dismissing it) was never affected either
# way and remains fully meaningful to compare.
IMPORT ../common/wipe_and_prep.seq
APPMSG --int 10029=0

APPMSG --string 10000="$(printf 'Quick Timer\0375\0371')"
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10021=1 10022=5
SLEEP 1
SLEEP 7
SCREENSHOT alarm_just_fired_elapsed_near_0
SLEEP 5
SCREENSHOT alarm_elapsed_should_now_read_about_5s
TAP down
SLEEP 1
SCREENSHOT dismissed
